# Does this exchange really win?
teaches: ["calculation/counting","calculation/in-between-move"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How do you count attackers and defenders on a square? Why can a trade that looks equal lose material? What is an in-between move, and why does it break your count?"
next: {"title":"How far ahead can you see?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/04-how-far-ahead-can-you-see.cast"}

## Warm-up: what did his move do?
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/2np4/2b1p3/4n3/1BN2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxe4]}
      - {fen: r1b2rk1/ppp2ppp/3p4/8/2B3nq/2NP4/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [h3], arrows: [{from: g4, to: h2}, {from: h4, to: h2}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    safety: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: does this exchange really win? First, two warm-up puzzles from the last two days.
    draw position

B: Black's knight just took my pawn on e4, and it hits my knight. I'd move my knight.
    highlight piece_c3

A: Ask what his move left behind. Who guards that knight on e4?
    point at.ref piece_e4 gesture circle

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Count who guards the knight on e4.","Your knight on c3."] themes ["hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Knight takes e4: one attacker, no defender, so the knight was simply loose.
        wrong: Before you save your knight, count who guards his.

B: Nobody guarded it. A free knight.
    highlight piece_e4

A: Second one. Black's knight jumped to g4. What does it threaten?
    blocking false

    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    highlight piece_g4

B: His queen is in front of my pawns. Pawn to g3, and I kick her.
    point at.ref sq_g3 gesture tap

A: Then queen takes h2 is mate.
    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

A: The knight and the queen both hit h2, and only your king guards it.
    highlight piece_g4 piece_h4

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

    ask "Stop the threat." widget chess line true hints ["Block the queen's path to h2.","A pawn move."] themes ["threat"] voice a teaches ["safety/threats"]
        right: Pawn to h3: it shuts the queen off from h2 and attacks the knight on g4.
        wrong: Pawn to g3 still allows queen takes h2. What stands between the queen and h2?

B: It blocks the queen, and it kicks the knight too.
    highlight piece_h3 piece_g4

A: Both puzzles came down to one count: who attacks a square, and who guards it.
    point at.ref sq_h2 gesture circle

A: Today we make that count exact: red attacks, green guards.
    animate safety 1 duration 1.2

## Count the men, then the points
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1b2rk1/ppp1qppp/3p4/4n3/8/3P1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQR1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxe5, dxe5, Rxe5, Qxe5]}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/2npp3/8/2BnP3/2N1B3/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxd4, Nxd4, Qxd4]}
      - {fen: r3qrk1/1p2bppp/4pn2/3p4/8/2N2B2/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxd5, exd5, Bxd5, Nxd5, Qxd5]}
      - {fen: 5rk1/ppp1bppp/8/4pN2/3qP3/8/PPP2PPP/3Q1RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Qxd1, Nxe7+, Kh8, Rxd1]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    counts:
      - e5
      - d4
      - d5
sources:
    - id: zw
      title: Zwischenzug
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 'A zwischenzug (German: ''intermediate move'') is a forcing move played instead of the expected reply, usually a recapture; an in-between check is also called zwischenschach.'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zwischenzug

    card title "The count"

A: Now that you've counted one guard, count them all.
    draw position

A: Black's knight on e5 is attacked twice.
    highlight piece_e5

B: Twice! Knight takes, he takes back, rook takes. I win it.
    highlight piece_f3 piece_e1

A: Knight for knight is fair.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 2.4

A: But then the queen takes your rook.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 2.4

    animate plies_shown 0 duration 1

B: Two attackers, and two defenders too.
    draw count_e5

A: So count both sides. More attackers than defenders is the first test.
    highlight piece_d6 piece_e7

    erase count_e5

A: Here: two attackers against one defender.
    blocking false

    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    highlight piece_e3 piece_d1 piece_c6

    draw count_d4

    draw count_d4

B: Queen takes. She's my strongest piece.
    point at.ref piece_d1 gesture circle

    erase count_d4

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Send the cheapest piece in first.","Your bishop on e3."] themes ["counting"] voice a
        right: Bishop takes d4, the knight takes back, and the queen takes again: a bishop for two knights. The queen goes in last, when nothing can take her.
        wrong: If the queen takes first, the knight on c6 takes her. Which piece costs least?

A: Now three attackers against two defenders,
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: on the pawn on d5.
    draw count_d5

B: Three beats two. Every capture is one for one, and I end with the pawn.
    highlight piece_c3 piece_f3 piece_d1

A: Three attackers, two defenders: does knight takes d5 win the pawn?
    point at.ref sq_d5 gesture circle

    quiz "Three attackers, two defenders: does knight takes d5 win the pawn?" on_canvas true say_question false buttons_at.x 905 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: No. The defenders are a pawn and a knight, cheaper than your knight and bishop: you give six points and take five.
        wrong: Add up what each capture takes, and what it gives.
        * Yes: 3 beats 2
        + No: 6 for 5
        * Queen first

    erase count_d5

A: Play it out to the end.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 3.5

A: You took two pawns and a knight: five points.
    text t_took "took 5" #3f8a4f x 905 y 440 font_size 40

A: You gave a knight and a bishop: six.
    text t_gave "gave 6" #b5482e x 905 y 370 font_size 40

B: So the trades were one for one, but not point for point.
    highlight t_took t_gave

    erase t_took t_gave

A: Count the men to see if you can take. Count the points to see if you should.
    animate plies_shown 0 duration 1.5

A: One more thing breaks a count.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Black takes your queen.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.4

B: Queen for queen. Rook takes back, of course.
    point at.ref piece_f1 gesture circle

A: Your count assumed you take back at once. Look for a check first.
    highlight piece_f5

    pause 0.3

    ask "Before you take back." widget chess line true hints ["A capture that gives check.","Your knight on f5."] themes ["in-between move"] voice a
        right: Knight takes e7, check. The king has to step to h8, and only then do you take back the queen: a bishop up.
        wrong: The queen on d1 isn't going anywhere. What can you take with check first?

B: Knight takes with check, and I still take the queen. A bishop extra.
    highlight piece_e7

A: That's an in-between move, zwischenzug in German:
    text t_zw "zwischenzug" cites ["zw"] x 905 y 560 font_size 30

A: a forcing move slipped in before the recapture everyone expected.
    highlight piece_e7

    erase t_zw

A: So an exchange wins only if it wins at the end:
    fade position to 0.35

A: count the men, count the points, and look for checks in between.
    text c_men "men" x 905 y 440 font_size 40
    text c_points "points" x 905 y 375 font_size 40
    text c_checks "checks" x 905 y 310 font_size 40

## Your turn: count it out
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Five for five. You count like an accountant.
    good: Most of them. The rest come with practice.
    poor: Counting takes a few games to stick. Try them again tomorrow.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/ppp2ppp/2n5/3p4/3Pn3/1N3P2/PPP3PP/R1BQR1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [fxe4]}
      - {fen: 6k1/ppp1qppp/2n3b1/8/4N3/2N5/PPP2PPP/3Q2K1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Bxe4, Nxe4, Qxe4], flip: true}
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/p3ppbp/1p1pbnp1/2p5/8/2N3P1/PP1PPPBP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxa8, Qxa8]}
      - {fen: 3Q1rk1/ppp2ppp/8/4p3/3n4/8/PPP1BPPP/5RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Nxe2+, Kh1, Rxd8], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/3p1n2/4p3/2BPP3/2b2N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [bxc3]}
      - {fen: r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/3ppnn1/8/1bPP4/3BPN2/PP3PPP/RNBQ1RK1 w kq - 0 1}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now that you can count an exchange, it's your turn: five puzzles, scored.
    draw position

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Count, then send the cheapest piece in first.","Your pawn on f3."] themes ["counting"] voice a test d3
        right: Pawn takes e4: a pawn for a knight. If he takes back, the rook takes again.
        wrong: The knight on e4 is guarded by a pawn. Which of your attackers costs least?

B: I'd have taken with the rook.
    highlight piece_e1

A: Rook takes, pawn takes back: you give five for three.
    point at.ref piece_e4 gesture circle

A: Next, you're Black.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["Count attackers and defenders on e4.","Bishop first, queen last."] themes ["counting"] voice a test d3
        right: Bishop takes e4, knight takes back, queen takes: two attackers beat one defender, and the queen goes in last.
        wrong: If the queen takes first, the knight on c3 takes her. Which piece goes in first?

A: Next: count the points, not the men.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Look down the long diagonal.","Your bishop on g2."] themes ["counting"] voice a test d3
        right: Bishop takes the rook on a8. The queen takes back, but you gave three points and took five.
        wrong: One for one can still win, if yours is the cheaper piece. What can your bishop take?

A: You're Black again. White has just taken your queen.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Before you take back." widget chess line true hints ["A capture with check.","Your knight on d4."] themes ["in-between move"] voice a test d3
        right: Knight takes e2, check. The king steps to h1, and then the rook takes back the queen: a bishop up.
        wrong: The queen on d8 can wait. What can your knight take with check?

A: Last one, as White. Black has just taken your knight.
    blocking false

    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    highlight piece_c3

A: Is there a check first?
    point at.ref piece_c4 gesture circle

    pause 0.3

    ask "Find the best move." widget chess line true hints ["Bishop takes f7 is check. Who takes back?","Take back at once."] themes ["counting"] voice a test d3
        right: Pawn takes c3, at once. The only check, bishop takes f7, just loses the bishop to the rook: check first only when the check wins something.
        wrong: A check first only helps if it costs him something. What happens after bishop takes f7?

    fade position to 0.15

A: You scored {d3.score} of {d3.total}.
    results test d3 retry true

B: Whatever the score, I'll count before I capture now.
    fade position to 1

A: Now against the computer. One black piece here is loose.
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Win it within five moves.
    explore activity vs_computer goal.kind win_material goal.within 5 goal.amount 3 store g

A: So an exchange is a count you finish:
    fade position to 0.35

A: the men, then the points, then the checks in between.
    text c_men "men" x 905 y 470 font_size 40
    text c_points "points" x 905 y 405 font_size 40
    text c_checks "checks" x 905 y 340 font_size 40

    erase position c_men c_points c_checks

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 440 font_size 64

A: Then find its first mistake.
    text hw2 "1st mistake?" x 500 y 330 font_size 64

